WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.200 --> 00:00:03.335
Once, a machine learned to see.

2
00:00:05.608 --> 00:00:10.058
Its eye: four hundred light
cells, twenty by twenty.

3
00:00:10.358 --> 00:00:15.408
Behind it, wires, and racks
of dials that motors turned.

4
00:00:16.058 --> 00:00:22.316
It was the Mark I Perceptron, the
project of psychologist Frank Rosenblatt.

5
00:00:23.450 --> 00:00:28.213
Nobody gave it the rule.
Show it a shape; it guesses.

6
00:00:28.513 --> 00:00:34.325
Each lit cell tugs the needle through
the dials it feeds; the total decides.

7
00:00:34.625 --> 00:00:38.839
When it guesses wrong, motors
nudge the dials that had a say,

8
00:00:38.839 --> 00:00:40.684
a little, toward the right answer.

9
00:00:41.484 --> 00:00:46.789
Wrong, and wrong again, and
then, quite suddenly... right.

10
00:00:48.639 --> 00:00:52.035
It could learn only simple patterns.

11
00:00:52.035 --> 00:00:59.947
In 1969, Minsky and Papert showed just
how simple; for years, interest faded.

12
00:00:59.947 --> 00:01:04.939
Its descendants, today's neural
networks, still learn from mistakes.
